#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125588} :activity/id #uuid "13665af8-c926-5261-b3fc-925c516a2fd1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393 :topic/id #uuid "9babae1d-ca0d-5438-a2e4-a65dbba9f7ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1913} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125849 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1601d67-32bc-5796-a800-6e704f25f991" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5500 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "965fe16f-9c57-5058-9e32-90332314977c" :step/math-academy-id 34230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124426 :question/id #uuid "8914449a-3d6f-5847-9e94-3be0a2ccbc94" :question/math-academy-id "e-9178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "bd127c03-d025-5b21-ae93-e24b7aec916f" :step/math-academy-id 34231 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125850 :tutorial/content "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "78f635ff-e2e7-5c9f-9ec2-470187a45bc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5501 :tutorial/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "429ea8df-33bc-5c79-a0d6-97b71166cd98" :step/math-academy-id 34232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124427 :question/id #uuid "d8798f55-f005-5019-bf47-349e43237888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411617}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8ba24a30-9b4e-5e58-93b1-b999bb45fe16" :step/math-academy-id 34233 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124428 :question/id #uuid "2164d0ba-43d5-53c7-adf1-9e6810b316a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9180"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411615}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3865f17c-09ee-5b73-96f9-45f1dd985dd9" :step/math-academy-id 34234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125851 :tutorial/content "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n> *Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> converges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*\n\nIn other words, if [math]P[/math] is a regular [math]m \\times m[/math] stochastic matrix and [math]\\mathbf{q}_0 \\in \\mathbf{R}^m[/math] is an arbitrary (initial) stochastic vector, then [math]P[/math] has the unique steady state vector [math]\\mathbf{q}[/math] and \n\n\\[\nP^n \\mathbf{q}_0 \\to \\mathbf{q} \n\\]\n\nas [math]n \\to \\infty.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "255fadf0-9fc2-52d5-976b-015a97f52b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5502 :tutorial/title "Regular Stochastic Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "735b7b96-43fa-5c0e-b4b8-df7598a6610b" :step/math-academy-id 34235 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125855 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{aligned}]\\approx [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{aligned}]\\approx [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb9435c4-44ed-58ec-9c04-b8fed4403fcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6151 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7c429a3d-9dd8-5fe2-a7e7-cf0e9f515d1a" :step/math-academy-id 37332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125565}}] :activity/title "Steady-State Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124712}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411620} :activity/id #uuid "86878afe-0434-571a-8f3d-da4de43c4751" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393 :topic/id #uuid "9babae1d-ca0d-5438-a2e4-a65dbba9f7ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1913} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fa980f-8d16-5c0b-adc6-32aaa0351649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411597} {:answer/id #uuid "3f726a36-e309-5b98-8554-2e43c91e6dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411601} {:answer/id #uuid "47f65641-4908-5c8e-b21e-1a448edd51ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411602} {:answer/id #uuid "49e05de2-77df-5db3-a385-91d68a9be627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411603} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d1b46f-e211-5172-be37-ae8796943205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411601} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e744f0f-1893-571c-a245-886e0be7796d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15361c81-aace-5a26-b7c5-2e82d38f451f" :question/math-academy-id "q-135349" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a+b-c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.9 & 0.4 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.9 & 0.3 & 0 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & -0.8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 19 & -19 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 19 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3}-8{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f47d0d7-0048-532c-8ccd-1d7c100cde28" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d26e28b-a37c-5f8e-b505-641bddc3ac1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "82\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411598} {:answer/id #uuid "13e7a544-2b8e-535c-9dbd-150b4b2182d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411599} {:answer/id #uuid "62af4b1d-dead-5e6e-8055-bd94bce27217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411605} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2be5c6-2ea1-5fdd-a19b-1ca6e9db49af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411608} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0e3cc-f79a-5b7f-a8f4-e19e89af1d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411609} :answer-field/id #uuid "f22ac3a1-3b1b-5c02-9aac-e44227bf9c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbc10db1-83ee-5f40-90d1-b581f73d6e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-135412" :question/problem "A study determines how customers' yearly preferences change between the LB and SB telephone companies. The table below describes the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **LB** | **SB** |\n| To: | **LB** | $0$ | $0.25$ |\n| **SB** | $1$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $100%$ of the customers who currently use the LB company will choose the SB company next year, while $25%$ of the customers who currently use the SB company will use the LB company next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $100\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, determine the number of customers who will eventually use the SB company." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.25 \\\\ 1 & 0.75\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 & 0.187\\,5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.812\\,5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.25 \\\\ 1 & 0.75\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.25 \\\\ 1 & -0.25\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -0.25 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0.25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the customers will eventually use the SB company. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this company is\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 100\\,000=80\\,000.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a72fda11-1a51-5e4f-8796-9dbea4da037a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2993011b-20a1-5970-adb0-721cadaaedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411600} {:answer/id #uuid "4ced8491-b081-5ef8-90f8-3698d8dd8753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411604} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa79523-2a8b-5ac4-a74a-e388c50d151f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411606} {:answer/id #uuid "7b481987-958d-5896-baf1-928789c0b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411607} {:answer/id #uuid "e7535899-b380-5240-8de6-34d4d073db6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411604} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46cc03c-be0d-5f3c-966b-7d8ddd1839d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62944003-1237-5928-b405-2159dbe448f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135308" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d2495fcf-fbe9-534c-8f3a-89d05a93dce9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411618}}] :activity/title "Steady-State Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411596}]}
